You do not have to keep this layering in your head; the build keeps it for you.
Each layer is its own module with a one-way import table,
so a reach across layers is a compile error, not a comment a reviewer has to catch.
The diagram above is the map, and the
[architecture](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md)
is the authoritative account of every layer.

## The protocol core

The core owns the protocol and nothing else. It is synchronous, holds no sockets, and
starts no background work, which is what lets it be tested deterministically and
dropped into any host without dragging a runtime along.

- **foundation** owns the primitives every other layer shares. A `Frame` has one
  visible owner, a `FrameView` borrows bytes for a bounded scope, and IDs are plain
  values.
- **model** is the codec-neutral CapTP vocabulary: references, answers, arguments,
  and resolutions, all defined independently of the bytes that carried them.
- **codec** turns model values into wire bytes and back.
  [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) is the Goblins-canonical profile and
  [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second codec; the
  [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page covers both. Sibling codecs cannot import each
  other, so neither can quietly grow a dependency on the other's shape.
- **session** is the authority. It owns the import, export, and answer tables,
  with the GC reference counts that release entries from them, and it decides
  what is valid. A codec never gets to decide whether a reference or a release
  is allowed.
- **engine** is the caller-owned `Peer`. You feed it inbound frames, call `step`,
  drain the outbound frames, and read the events. It dispatches a session and does
  nothing more.

## Above the core

The framework-shaped pieces sit on top of the core, never inside it.

- **actors** is a Goblins-shaped `Vat`. You spawn a local object, call it near with
  `$`, send it eventually with `<-`, and observe the promise that comes back. It
  adds no protocol semantics the engine lacks; it is the friendlier face on the
  substrate.
- **driver/core** is the sans-io multi-session CapTP serve core (`Served`, the
  byte-fed `Host`). It owns identity, `engine.Node`, vows, and crossed hellos, and
  the build bans sockets there so they cannot creep back in.
- **net** is the one place `std.Io` enters. A netlayer is a contract
  the core dispatches against rather than a privileged built-in, so a transport is a
  resident you register. Four ship today: cleartext TCP, keyed-tcp (same sockets,
  key-derived designator), an in-process switchboard for whole conversations
  inside one process, and [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/)'s encrypted QUIC as a
  separate optional artifact. The
  [Transports]($link.page('transports')) page walks all four.

> **Vat**: one single-threaded container of objects, the unit
> [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and
> the wider ocap world organize around. Every call into a vat runs as one *turn*,
> start to finish, before the next begins, so an object never sees a half-applied
> state and you reason about it the way you reason about a single thread.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)**: two ways to invoke an object,
> borrowed from the [E language](http://erights.org/). A near-call runs now and returns the result, because
> the object lives in your own vat. An eventual-send queues the call for a later turn
> and hands back a promise; it is also the only way to call across a session, since a
> partner's object is never in your vat.

## The surfaces

Two surfaces carry pancake out to the people building on it, and the
[Surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')) page is the consumer's-eye view of both.

- **c_abi** is the deliberate public surface: opaque
  handles, fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len` views, callbacks, and explicit
  release, so a consumer can wrap it well without knowing any Zig. The host archive
  (`libpancake_driver`) is the multi-session CapTP byte-fed face bindings wrap.
- **driver** is the native Zig application surface above `driver/core`. It offers
  one-session `Session`, multi-session CapTP `Bridge` + Host over a
  completion loop (`net.TcpLoop` / iroh Backend), and the byte-fed C ABI host.

The [bindings]($link.page('bindings')) are that same application surface once more, in
Rust, TypeScript, Python, and Racket. Each consumes the C ABI and presents it in its own
idiom, which is why an app reads like an app no matter the language.
